The program-scope threshold is 500 kW. Facilities below 500 kW, whether commercial or residential in customer<br>
class, are routed to the Simplified Small-Systems Process and are not administered by the DER Program.<br>
§3.1 Determination of aggregate nameplate capacity<br>
Aggregate nameplate capacity is the sum of the manufacturer-rated ac output ratings of all generating equipment<br>
interconnected, or proposed to be interconnected, at a single point of common coupling. Manufacturer datasheets<br>
govern; derated or "operating" figures do not substitute for nameplate except as provided in §3.6.<br>
§3.2 Point of common coupling<br>
The point of common coupling is the point at which the DER facility is electrically connected to the Company''s<br>
distribution system, as further defined in IEEE 1547-2018. The PCC is distinct from the point of interconnection (POI),<br>
the latter being the physical demarcation of Company-owned protective and metering equipment.<br>
§3.3 Determination on the basis of application<br>
Classification is determined on the facility as filed and as physically configured on site. Filing captions, project titles,<br>
and cover-letter characterizations do not alter classification; the connection diagram, the equipment schedule, and<br>
the address of record govern.<br>
§3.4 Aggregation<br>
Co-located DER behind the same point of common coupling counts toward one site total.<br>
Previously interconnected legacy equipment counts toward the aggregate regardless of the vintage of its permission<br>
to operate, regardless of whether it predates any current Alleniom standard, and regardless of the sequence or form<br>
in which subsequent applications are submitted at the same PCC. The aggregate governs the facility class and the<br>
resulting program-scope determination.<br>
An applicant proposing a new DER at a premises that already hosts DER shall identify the existing equipment on the<br>
application form and shall submit a one-line diagram showing the electrical relationship of the proposed equipment,<br>
the existing equipment, the customer load, the customer meter, and the PCC. Interconnection Studies verifies against<br>
the Company existing-DER register.<br>
§3.5 Shared-POI eligibility<br>
A new DER may share an existing POI only where the combined facility, including legacy equipment, can be brought<br>
to current monitoring/control standards; otherwise a separate POI and a new application are required. Where the<br>
existing system operates behind the meter, a new DER may instead be interconnected front-of-the-meter under its<br>
own service and POI, and is then evaluated on its own capacity without aggregation.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>
Page 4<br>
DIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>
The two dispositions available under §3.5 are:<br>
1.  Shared POI, aggregated. The combined facility (legacy equipment plus proposed equipment) is brought to<br>
current monitoring/control standards as prescribed by TCS-02 and by Table 5-1 at the aggregate class. The<br>
applicant is responsible for the retrofit and for demonstrating retrofit feasibility to Interconnection Studies. Where<br>
feasibility is confirmed, the application proceeds at the aggregate capacity at the shared POI.<br>
2.  Separate POI, non-aggregated front-of-the-meter alternative. Where the existing system operates behind<br>
the meter and the applicant elects to interconnect the new DER front-of-the-meter under a separate service and<br>
its own POI, the new DER is evaluated on its own capacity, without aggregation with the legacy equipment.<br>
Class placement, review path, and Table 5-1 category are determined from the new DER''s capacity alone.<br>
Retrofit feasibility of legacy equipment is a factual question the Company will not decide from records alone; the<br>
applicant or its authorized developer must confirm feasibility in writing. Absent that confirmation, the shared-POI path<br>
under §3.5(1) is not available and the application is redirected to §3.5(2) or withdrawn.<br>
§3.6 Controlled-capacity recognition<br>
A limit below nameplate is recognized only with a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification plus an approved Control<br>
Narrative — sufficient in themselves for recognition. Where the site''s arrangement includes utility equipment, the<br>
recognized limit is enforced as applicable: telemetry monitoring, and on pure-generation sites the POI recloser''s ANSI<br>
32 directional-power element set to the recognized limit and tripping on violation.<br>
Required submissions. To have a controlled operating limit recognized in place of aggregate nameplate for<br>
program-scope and study purposes, the applicant shall submit both of the following:<br>
1.  A valid Power Control System (PCS) certification issued under UL 1741 (including Supplement SB where<br>
applicable) and/or UL 3141, identifying the specific equipment arrangement at the applicant''s site and the<br>
setpoint range.<br>
2.  An approved Control Narrative — a written engineering document describing how the site''s control system<br>
enforces the requested operating limit, including the sensed quantities, the response actions, the response<br>
times, the interlocks, and any allowable modes and their setpoint ceilings.<br>
These two documents, taken together, are sufficient in themselves to establish recognition of the controlled limit.<br>
Recognition establishes the capacity at which the facility is reviewed, classified, and studied.<br>
Enforcement backstops as arrangement provides. Where the site''s arrangement includes utility equipment, the<br>
recognized limit is enforced by the arrangement''s existing equipment as follows:<br>
 Telemetry monitoring. For sites at or above the TCS-02 threshold, the Company''s RTU-to-SCADA telemetry<br>
monitors real-power export at the point of interconnection against the recognized limit. Sustained deviation<br>
triggers investigation under TCS-02 and may result in a hold on permission to operate.<br>
 POI recloser directional-power element (pure-generation sites). On pure-generation sites — sites at which<br>
no customer load is served behind the point of common coupling — where a utility POI recloser is installed per<br>
Table 5-1 and DPS-07, the recloser''s ANSI 32 directional-power element is set to the recognized limit and trips<br>
the site on violation. The ANSI 32 element is inherently available in modern reclosers and does not require<br>
additional utility hardware.<br>
Enforcement provisions apply only as the site''s arrangement provides them. Their absence at a particular site does<br>
not reopen the recognition determination once the two required submissions are on file.<br>
No controlled limit without submissions. In the absence of both a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification and<br>
an approved Control Narrative, nameplate governs for classification, program-scope determination, and study.<br>
Certified Equipment List. CEL-2025-Q2 restates this section in its front matter. Listing of a PCS on the current CEL<br>
does not by itself constitute recognition of a controlled limit for a particular site; the applicant''s site-specific<br>
certification and Control Narrative are always required.</p>
<hr>
<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Page 5<br>
